# Code Style

Use blank lines to separate meaningful logical units of code, but prefer compact code by default.

Do NOT add blank lines between ordinary sequential statements that belong to the same logical operation.

Prefer:

```ts
const customer = await getCustomer(customerId);
const appointment = await getAppointment(appointmentId);
```

over:

```ts
const customer = await getCustomer(customerId);

const appointment = await getAppointment(appointmentId);
```

The main case where a blank line should be added is when a control-flow block or multi-line statement is followed by an independent statement.

## Control-flow blocks followed by independent statements

When a control-flow block is followed by an independent statement, add a blank line after the closing `}`.

Prefer:

```ts
if (isAnySpecialist && !selectedMemberId) {
  return activeStaff.some((staff) =>
    isAddonAvailableForMember(addon.staff, staff.member.id),
  );
}

return isAddonAvailableForMember(addon.staff, selectedMemberId);
```

over:

```ts
if (isAnySpecialist && !selectedMemberId) {
  return activeStaff.some((staff) =>
    isAddonAvailableForMember(addon.staff, staff.member.id),
  );
}
return isAddonAvailableForMember(addon.staff, selectedMemberId);
```

Another example:

```ts
if (!customer) {
  return null;
}

const appointments = await getAppointments(customer.id);
```

Another:

```ts
if (hasChanges) {
  await saveChanges();
}

await invalidateCache();
```

The blank line communicates that the control-flow branch is complete and the following statement is a separate logical step.

## Early returns

Use a blank line between a guard/early-return block and the main logic that follows.

Prefer:

```ts
if (!user) {
  return null;
}

const company = await getCompany(user.companyId);
return company;
```

For multiple closely related guard clauses, keep them compact:

```ts
if (!user) {
  return null;
}
if (!user.companyId) {
  return null;
}

const company = await getCompany(user.companyId);
return company;
```

Do not add blank lines between ordinary sequential statements:

```ts
const customer = await getCustomer(customerId);
const service = await getService(serviceId);
const duration = service.duration;

return buildAppointment(customer, service, duration);
```

## If / else / switch / try-catch

Do NOT add blank lines between parts of the same control-flow construct.

Prefer:

```ts
if (condition) {
  doSomething();
} else {
  doSomethingElse();
}
```

```ts
if (status === "pending") {
  handlePending();
} else if (status === "confirmed") {
  handleConfirmed();
} else {
  handleOther();
}

sendNotification();
```

```ts
try {
  await operation();
} catch (error) {
  handleError(error);
}

await continueProcessing();
```

Do not put blank lines between `try`, `catch`, or `finally`.

## Multi-line statements

A multi-line statement followed by an independent statement should have a blank line.

Prefer:

```ts
const availableSlots = slots.filter(
  (slot) => slot.start >= startTime && slot.end <= endTime,
);

const firstAvailableSlot = availableSlots[0];
```

Prefer:

```ts
const hasAvailableMember = activeStaff.some((staff) =>
  isAddonAvailableForMember(addon.staff, staff.member.id),
);

return hasAvailableMember;
```

However, ordinary consecutive statements should remain compact:

```ts
const activeStaff = staff.filter((staff) => staff.active);
const availableStaff = activeStaff.filter((staff) =>
  isStaffAvailable(staff, date),
);
const firstAvailableStaff = availableStaff[0];
```

Do not insert blank lines just because a statement spans multiple lines.

## Function declarations

Separate function declarations with a blank line.

Prefer:

```ts
function getCustomer(id: string) {
  return customers.get(id);
}

function getAppointment(id: string) {
  return appointments.get(id);
}
```

Inside a function, use blank lines only when they communicate a meaningful logical boundary.

Prefer:

```ts
async function createAppointment(input: CreateAppointmentInput) {
  const customer = await findCustomer(input.customerId);
  const service = await getService(input.serviceId);

  const appointment = buildAppointment(customer, service, input);

  await saveAppointment(appointment);

  return appointment;
}
```

Here blank lines separate meaningful stages, but related setup statements remain together.

## Variables and sequential operations

Keep related sequential statements together.

Prefer:

```ts
const customer = await getCustomer(customerId);
const service = await getService(serviceId);
const duration = service.duration;
const price = service.price;

const appointment = buildAppointment({
  customer,
  service,
  duration,
  price,
});
```

Do NOT turn every variable or operation into its own paragraph.

Avoid:

```ts
const customer = await getCustomer(customerId);

const service = await getService(serviceId);

const duration = service.duration;

const price = service.price;
```

## Nested blocks

Do not add a blank line merely because a nested block ends.

Prefer:

```ts
if (customer) {
  if (customer.isActive) {
    processCustomer(customer);
  }

  updateCustomerMetadata(customer);
}

sendNotification();
```

The blank line after the outer control-flow block separates it from the independent `sendNotification()` operation.

## Array callbacks

Keep short callbacks compact.

Prefer:

```ts
const activeStaff = staff.filter((staff) => staff.active);
const availableStaff = activeStaff.filter((staff) =>
  isStaffAvailable(staff, date),
);
```

Do not add blank lines between the callback and the surrounding operation unless there is a meaningful logical boundary.

## General rule

Default to compact code.

Add a blank line when it communicates one of these boundaries:

1. A control-flow block has finished and independent code follows.
2. An early-return/guard section has finished and the main logic follows.
3. A multi-line operation has finished and an independent operation follows.
4. A function or other top-level declaration ends and another declaration begins.
5. There is a clear transition between meaningful stages of a larger operation.

Do NOT add blank lines:

- Between ordinary sequential statements.
- Between related variable declarations.
- Between related API calls or setup operations.
- Between `if` and `else`.
- Between `try` and `catch`.
- Merely because a statement or expression spans multiple lines.
- Mechanically after every closing `}`.

When uncertain, prefer fewer blank lines.
